---
// Research index: a directory of protocols, grouped by sector.
//
// Not a reverse-chronological feed — research is a matrix of
// (protocol x version x mechanism) revised over months, and the useful view of
// a matrix is the grid, including its empty cells.
import LayoutDefault from '~/layouts/LayoutDefault.astro'
import SiteSeo from '~/components/SiteSeo.astro'
import ProtocolCard from '~/components/research/ProtocolCard.astro'
import { SECTOR_LABEL, getProtocolsBySector } from '~/utils'

const bySector = await getProtocolsBySector()
const total = Array.from(bySector.values()).flat()
const studied = total.filter(p => p.noteCount > 0).length
---

<LayoutDefault>
  <SiteSeo
    slot="seo"
    title="Research"
    desc="Protocol mechanism research — math and engineering decomposition."
  />

  <section class="prose">
    <h2 class="post-title">Research</h2>

    <p class="lead">
      The object of study here is the engine, not the market around it. Most protocol writing
      explains what a protocol offers and who might want it; these notes take the matching
      mechanism apart and state it formally.
    </p>

    <p>Every note works along three axes, and each has to come out as something checkable:</p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Algorithmic design</strong> — the invariant, and the closed form that answers a
        quote. What must hold across an operation, and what that forces the price to be.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Market structure design</strong> — what the mechanism obliges participants to do.
        Who supplies, who takes, what each side is being paid or charged for, and what the design
        makes impossible.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cost design</strong> — where clean algebra meets <code>uint256</code> and finite
        gas: fixed-point format, which direction a quantity rounds, and whom that rounding favours.
        The divergence is always deliberate.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Not discussed: demand, adoption, user segments, narrative. Volume is a consequence of engine
      design rather than an input to it, so it belongs downstream of every question asked here. A
      claim only counts once it is anchored to a pinned commit.
    </p>

    <p class="op-55 text-3.5">
      {studied} of {total.length} protocols have notes. The greyed tiles are the queue.
    </p>

    {
      Array.from(bySector).map(([sector, protocols]) => (
        <section class="mt-10">
          <h3 class="post-title mb-4"># {SECTOR_LABEL[sector] ?? sector}</h3>
          <div class="protocol-grid">
            {protocols.map(p => (
              <ProtocolCard
                id={p.id}
                name={p.data.name}
                logo={p.data.logo}
                chains={p.data.chains}
                mechanism={p.data.mechanism}
                noteCount={p.noteCount}
              />
            ))}
          </div>
        </section>
      ))
    }
  </section>
</LayoutDefault>

<style>
  .protocol-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(17rem, 1fr));
    gap: 0.875rem;
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
  }
</style>
